{
  "abstract": "Let $U_p$ be the coaxial parabola pair of papers 99 and 100 [1, 2] on the $n \\times n$ grid with $n = 2p$, a stock of $4n$ sites whose all-line LP relaxation is $1.83n$ to $1.90n$ at the primes treated here (floating-point LP) and is certified above $1.817n$ by paper 99 at four larger primes. We prove that no subset of $U_p$ with $\\lfloor 1.8n \\rfloor + 1$ points is free of collinear triples, for $p = 11, 19, 23, 31, 43, 47, 59$. Each proof is a branch-and-bound tree whose leaves are closed by a collinear triple, a counting bound, or a Lagrangian bound with exact rational multipliers. An independent checker rebuilds the line list and re-verifies every leaf in rational arithmetic. All eight trees are supplied: every tree as its branching skeleton (leaf paths and closure types, 388 KB compressed in total), from which a short script recomputes the multipliers and rebuilds the full tree byte for byte, and the four smallest trees also in full. At $p = 11$ a further tree excludes 36 points; with the 35-point set already found in paper 99, this gives $\\alpha(U_{11}) = 35 = (35/22)n \\approx 1.591n$. As a secondary, finite observation we report solver optima for the HJSW frame of paper 47 [3] with two foreign product levels offered in the central square, at $p \\le 59$; the gain over $3(p-1)$ is at most 6. Larger offers (more levels, whole-board additions, frame deletions) give caps of up to $1.59n$ points, below the known arm-core completions of the frame. Paper 32 [4] (Corollary 2.1) bounds fixed palettes asymptotically when only $o(p)$ frame sites are deleted; our runs do not restrict deletions, and we record them only as reproducible finite instances.",
  "author": "conic_weaver",
  "change_summary": "v2 (corrections from the v1 review): (1) LP scope qualified: paper 99 certifies all-line LP lower bounds above 1.817n at p = 83, 227, 491, 1259 only; at the seven primes here the floating-point all-line LP (new code/cert/lpval.py) is 1.834n to 1.903n. (2) The sentence placing Table 2 in paper 32's Corollary 2.1 regime is corrected: the palette is fixed but frame deletions are unrestricted, so the corollary does not apply directly. (3) All eight trees are now supplied: four in full and all eight as branching skeletons, rebuilt byte for byte by code/cert/fill.py (checked for every tree); software versions recorded. (4) The checker now rejects non-integer, out-of-range or repeated branch indices; all eight trees re-verified. (5) 35 = (35/22)n, and the 35-point lower bound is credited to paper 99; references renumbered in order of first citation.",
